 
<section xml:id="exercises">
  <title>Exercises</title> 
<subsection>
      <title>Vectors, Independence, and Span</title>

      <exercise>
      <introduction>
      <p>Consider the following vectors 
      <me>\vec{a} = (x+y, x+z, y+z)</me> and 
      <me>\vec{b} = (x, 1+x+y, x+y+z)</me>.
      Do the following:</p></introduction> 
      <task> 
      <p>Rewrite <m>\vec{a}</m> in the form <m>x\vec{v}_{1} + y \vec{v}_{2} + z \vec{v}_{3} + \vec{v}_{4}</m>, where
      <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \vec{v}_{2}, \vec{v}_{3},</m> and <m>\vec{v}_{4}</m> all have entries in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.</p>
      </task>
      <task>
      <p>Rewrite <m>\vec{b}</m> in the form <m>x\vec{v}_{1} + y \vec{v}_{2} + z \vec{v}_{3} + \vec{v}_{4}</m>, where
      <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \vec{v}_{2}, \vec{v}_{3},</m> and <m>\vec{v}_{4}</m> all have entries in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.</p>
      </task>
     <task>
      <p>Rewrite <m>\vec{a} + \vec{b}</m> in the form <m>x\vec{v}_{1} + y \vec{v}_{2} + z \vec{v}_{3} + \vec{v}_{4}</m>, where
      <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \vec{v}_{2}, \vec{v}_{3},</m> and <m>\vec{v}_{4}</m> all have entries in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.</p>
      </task>
      </exercise>

      <p> We say vectors are <b>linearly independent</b> if there is no linear combination
      of them with nonzero coefficients that equals <m>\vec{0}</m>. For example, <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(0,1)</m> are linearly independent,
      since if <m>x(1,0) + y(0,1) = (0,0)</m>, then <m>x = y = 0</m>. However, <m> (1,0) </m>, <m>(0,1)</m>, and 
      <m>(2,2)</m> are not linear independent, because <m> 2(1,0)+ 2(0,1) - (2,2) = (0,0)</m>. </p>
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Consider the vector equation:
            <me>x_{1} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} + x_{2} \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1\\ 1\end{pmatrix} + x_{3} \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\\ 0 \end{pmatrix}  = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <p>Rewrite this is a system of linear equations.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>Solve the linear system.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>
            Based on your solution to part (b), are <m>(1,0,1)</m>, <m>(0,1,1)</m>, and <m>(1,1,0)</m> linearly independent?
          </p>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>
          Is <m>(1,0,1,0)</m> in the span of <m>(1,1,0,0)</m>, <m>(1,0,0,1)</m>, and <m>(0,1,1,0)</m>? Explain why or why not.
        </p>
      </exercise>

</subsection>
<subsection>
<title> Matrix Form </title>
 <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Consider the matrix
            <me>A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 \\ 3 &amp;3&amp; 4 &amp; 4\end{pmatrix}</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Consider the vectors
            <me>\vec{v}_{1} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}, \vec{v}_{2} =\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 1 \\ -3\end{pmatrix}</me>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <p>Compute <m>A \vec{v}_{1}</m>.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>Compute <m>\vec{v}_{1} + \vec{v}_{2}</m>.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>Compute <m>2 \vec{v}_{1}</m>.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>Compute <m>2A \vec{v}_{1}</m> and <m>A (2 \vec{v}_{1})</m>.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>Compute <m>A \vec{v}_{1} +A\vec{v}_{2}</m> and <m>A(\vec{v}_{1} + \vec{v}_{2})</m>.</p>
        </task>
      </exercise>


      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Consider the matrix equation:
            <me>\begin{pmatrix}
    1 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\
    1 &amp; 0 &amp; 1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y\\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</me>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <p>Rewrite this as a system of linear equations.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>Solve the system of linear equations.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>
            Use your solution to describe the plane spanned by <m>(1,1,0)</m> and <m>(1,0,1)</m> by a linear equation.
          </p>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>
          Consider the <m>2</m>-plane in <m>\mathbb{R}^{4}</m> spanned by <m>(1,0,1,0)</m> and <m>(0,1,0,1)</m>. Write this plane as a solution set to a system of linear equations.
        </p>
      </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>    
